{"cuisine/filipino-american-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/filipino-american-cuisine","n":"Filipino-American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Filipino-American cuisine has been present in the United States since ever Filipinos first immigrated there, but only recently has Filipino food become more widely popular. Filipino food has evolved by adapting other cultures' food practices into their own, or borrowing other food concepts into their own."],["Filipinos debuted their food as they immigrated to the United States by presenting it in catering and opening the Philippines' most popular food chain, Jollibee. There is also a long list of different Filipino dishes that represent Filipino Americans."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"filipino-american-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino-American_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Filipino-American Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jennifer M. Aranas","note":"Traditional Filipino recipes with contemporary American touches, by a Chicago chef.","url":"https://www.tuttlepublishing.com/philippines/the-filipino-american-kitchen-9780804851305","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780804851688"},{"name":"Filipinx: Heritage Recipes from the Diaspora","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Angela Dimayuga and Ligaya Mishan, 2021","note":"Filipino American cooking from a New York chef raised in the Bay Area.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781419750380","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781419750380"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f0/Ube_crinkles_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Ube_crinkles_%28cropped%29.jpg","w":500,"h":419,"by":"Judgefloro","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ube_crinkles_(cropped).jpg"}},"cuisine/afghan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/afghan-cuisine","n":"Afghan cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Afghanistan","intro":[["Afghan cuisine is the set of foods and culinary styles which are associated with Afghan culture. The cuisine is halal and mainly based on mutton, beef and poultry with rice and Afghan bread. Accompanying these are common vegetables and dairy products, such as milk, yogurt, whey, and fresh and dried fruits such as apples, apricots, grapes, bananas, oranges, plums, pomegranates, sweet melons, and raisins. The diet of most Afghans revolves around rice-based dishes, while various forms of naan are consumed with most meals. Tea is generally consumed daily in large quantities, and is a major part of hospitality. The culinary specialties reflect the nation's ethnic and geographic diversity. The national dish of Afghanistan is Kabuli pulao, a rice dish cooked with raisins, carrots, nuts, and lamb or beef."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"afghan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afghan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Kabuli pulao","dish/kabuli-pulao"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Thali","dish/thali"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Afghan Culture Unveiled","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"afghancultureunveiled.com","note":"Afghan-American Humaira Ghilzai's blog on Afghan food and culture.","url":"https://www.afghancultureunveiled.com/humaira-ghilzai/afghancooking/2013/01/our-aushak-recipe-in-print-food52.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Afghan Cooks","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"afghancooks.com","note":"Recipe blog dedicated to traditional Afghan dishes.","url":"https://afghancooks.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Best of Afghan Cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Zarghuna Adel","note":"Traditional Afghan home recipes from Hippocrene Books.","url":"https://www.hippocrenebooks.com/store/p498/TheBestofAfghanCooking.html","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780781814430"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Ocimum","ingredient/ocimum"],["Rose syrup","ingredient/rose-syrup"],["Rose water","ingredient/rose-water"],["Sweet basil","ingredient/sweet-basil"],["Vermicelli","ingredient/vermicelli"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Nut_(fruit)","ingredient/nut-fruit"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Raisins","ingredient/raisins"],["Steamed rice","ingredient/steamed-rice"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"],["Citric acid","ingredient/citric-acid"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Afghan cooking","count":2,"links":[["Hazara cuisine","cuisine/hazara-cuisine"],["Pashtun cuisine","cuisine/pashtun-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/c1/Afghan_cuisine.jpg/500px-Afghan_cuisine.jpg","w":500,"h":449,"by":"Officer","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afghan_cuisine.jpg"}},"cuisine/alutiiq-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/alutiiq-foodways","n":"Alutiiq foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Kodiak Island, Alaska Peninsula and Prince William Sound","names":"Alutiiq, Sugpiaq","intro":[["The Alutiiq, or Sugpiaq, live from Prince William Sound to Kodiak Island and the Alaska Peninsula. Like their Unangax̂ neighbors, their food is tied to the water: ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," smoked and dried, halibut, seal, shellfish and seaweed, with berries and greens from the land."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"alutiiq-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/anishinaabe-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/anishinaabe-foodways","n":"Anishinaabe foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Great Lakes region","names":"Anishinaabe, Ojibwe, Odawa, Potawatomi","intro":[["Anishinaabe oral tradition tells of a journey west to the place where food grows on water. That food is ",["manoomin","ingredient/wild-rice"],", wild rice, and it still feeds Ojibwe communities in Minnesota, Wisconsin and Michigan in body and spirit. At harvest, ricers pole or paddle small boats through the beds and knock the ripe grain in with cedar sticks, going back again and again as it ripens."],["Late winter is sugarbush time, when families return to the same maple stands, some kept for generations, to boil sap into ",["syrup","ingredient/maple-syrup"]," and sugar. Maple sugar seasons food, and sugar cakes molded into people, animals and moons are given as gifts."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"St. Croix Chippewa Indians of Wisconsin, Manoomin","url":"https://stcroixojibwe-nsn.gov/manoomin/"},{"name":"Michigan Sea Grant, Manoomin (wild rice)","url":"https://www.michiganseagrant.org/topics/ecosystems-and-habitats/native-species-and-biodiversity/mano"},{"name":"National Park Service, Grand Portage, Iskigamizigan (sugarbush)","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/iskigamizigan-sugarbush.htm"},{"name":"Native Harvest, White Earth Land Recovery Project","url":"https://www.nativeharvest.com/blogs/news/tagged/sugar-bush"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"anishinaabe-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Bannock (Indigenous American food)","dish/bannock-indigenous-american-food"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Native Harvest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"nativeharvest.com","note":"Wild rice and maple syrup from the White Earth Reservation in Minnesota.","url":"https://www.nativeharvest.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Original Local: Indigenous Foods, Stories, and Recipes from the Upper Midwest","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Heid E. Erdrich, 2013","note":"Indigenous foods, stories and recipes from the Upper Midwest.","url":"https://www.mnhs.org/mnhspress/books/original-local","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780873518949"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/51/Inuit_bannock.JPG/500px-Inuit_bannock.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"CambridgeBayWeather","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inuit_bannock.JPG","of":"Bannock (Indigenous American food)"}},"dish/bannu-pulao":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bannu-pulao","n":"Bannu pulao","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Pakistani cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bannu pulao or Bannu beef pulao, also called Banuse pulao (Pashto: بنوڅۍ پلاوو), is a traditional mixed rice dish from the Bannu district of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan. It is made with beef, rice, spices, and stock. The beef is cooked with bones and marrow, which gives the dish a rich and flavorful taste. The rice is cooked separately with ghee, salt, and whole spices. The dish is served with a garnish of onions, green chilies, and lemon wedges."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bannu-pulao"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bannu_pulao"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pakistan Eats","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pakistaneats.substack.com","note":"Maryam Jillani's newsletter on Pakistani food, recipes and food culture.","url":"https://pakistaneats.substack.com/p/pakistani-dishes-for-beginners","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Pakistani cuisine","links":[["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Pakistani rice dishes","dish/pakistani-rice-dishes"],["Paratha roll","dish/paratha-roll"],["Reshmi kabab","dish/reshmi-kabab"],["Tandoori roti","dish/tandoori-roti"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Zarda (food)","dish/zarda-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/a/a8/Bannu_Gull_Beeff_Pulao.jpg","w":500,"h":697,"by":"Nainsahil","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bannu_Gull_Beeff_Pulao.jpg"}},"dish/blabarssoppa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/blabarssoppa","n":"Blåbärssoppa","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Swedish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Blåbärssoppa is a Nordic fruit soup made from bilberries (European blueberries), which can be served cold or hot. It is sweet and contains starch, which gives it a fairly thick consistency. It is served either as soup or as a drink."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-blabarssoppa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bl%C3%A5b%C3%A4rssoppa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Bilberries","ingredient/bilberries"],["Bilberry","ingredient/bilberry"],["Potato starch","ingredient/potato-starch"],["Starch","ingredient/starch"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scandicuisine: Swedish recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scandicuisine.com","note":"Traditional Swedish recipes from Cecilia, a cook from a Finnish-Swedish family on Åland.","url":"https://scandicuisine.com/category/swedish-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Swedish cuisine","links":[["Biff à la Lindström","dish/biff-a-la-lindstrom"],["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Flying Jacob","dish/flying-jacob"],["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Hasselback potatoes","dish/hasselback-potatoes"],["Isterband","dish/isterband"],["Kebab pizza","dish/kebab-pizza"],["Korv stroganoff","dish/korv-stroganoff"],["Kroppkaka","dish/kroppkaka"],["Limpa","dish/limpa"],["Lutefisk","dish/lutefisk"],["Oatmeal ball","dish/oatmeal-ball"],["Ostkaka","dish/ostkaka"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"],["Surströmming","dish/surstromming"],["Swedish meatballs","dish/swedish-meatballs"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/be/Bl%C3%A5b%C3%A4rssoppa.jpg/500px-Bl%C3%A5b%C3%A4rssoppa.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Dnor","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bl%C3%A5b%C3%A4rssoppa.jpg"}},"dish/bollo":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bollo","n":"Bollo","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Latin American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A bollo is a bun, common in Latin America, made from corn, yuca, or potato. Variations are found in the cuisines of Colombia, Ecuador, and Panama. Corn and yuca bollos are an indigenous food of the Caribbean coast of Colombia and Panama, where they are boiled in leaves. This preparation is similar to the humita of the Andes, the hallaquita of Venezuela and the pamonha of Brazil."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bollo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bollo"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Latin American cuisine","cuisine/latin-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"]]},{"h":"More from Latin American cuisine","links":[["Buñuelo","dish/bunuelo"],["Frijoles negros","dish/frijoles-negros"],["Quince cheese","dish/quince-cheese"],["Rice and beans","dish/rice-and-beans"],["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/bc/Bollo_de_yuca.jpg/500px-Bollo_de_yuca.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jdvillalobos","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bollo_de_yuca.jpg"}},"ingredient/peanut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/peanut","n":"Peanut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 10 dishes across 6 cuisines","intro":[["The peanut (Arachis hypogaea), also known as the groundnut, goober (US, via Kikongo), goober pea, pindar (US, via Kikongo) or monkey nut (UK), is a legume crop grown mainly for its edible seeds, contained in underground pods. It is widely grown in the tropics and subtropics by small and large commercial producers, both as a grain legume and as an oil crop. Underground fruiting (geocarpy) is atypical among legumes, which led botanist Carl Linnaeus to name the species hypogaea, from Greek 'under the earth'."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-peanut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peanut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":6,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Oncom","dish/oncom"],["Rempeyek","dish/rempeyek"],["Sate padang","dish/sate-padang"],["Sate taichan","dish/sate-taichan"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Kung Pao chicken","dish/kung-pao-chicken"]]],["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Hotteok","dish/hotteok"]]],["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",[["Miyar zogale","dish/miyar-zogale"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Flying Jacob","dish/flying-jacob"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Pad thai","dish/pad-thai"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Groundnuts, excluding shelled","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":55399275,"top":[["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",18694760],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",10568891],["Nigeria","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",4268416],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",2756472],["Sudan","cuisine/sudanese-cuisine",2168017],["Myanmar","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",1713595],["Senegal","cuisine/senegalese-cuisine",1666814],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",1268868],["Guinea","cuisine/guinean-cuisine",946696],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",795089],["Chad","cuisine/chadian-cuisine",788511],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",773647],["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",664600],["Niger","cuisine/cuisine-of-niger",604438],["Burkina Faso","cuisine/burkinabe-cuisine",592260]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lime","ingredient/lime"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Cinnamomum verum","ingredient/cinnamomum-verum"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a4/Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg/500px-Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg","w":500,"h":743,"by":"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg"}},"ingredient/japanese-curry":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/japanese-curry","n":"Japanese curry","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Japanese curry is commonly served in three main forms: curry rice (カレーライス, karē raisu) (curry over white rice), curry udon (カレーうどん, karē udon) (curry over thick noodles), and curry bread (カレーパン, karē pan) (a curry-filled pastry). It is one of the most popular dishes in Japan. The very common curry rice dish is most often referred to simply as curry (カレー, karē)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-japanese-curry"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_curry"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/3/3b/Beef_curry_rice_003.jpg/500px-Beef_curry_rice_003.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Ocdp","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beef_curry_rice_003.jpg"}},"dish/duxelles":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/duxelles","n":"Duxelles","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Duxelles is a French cuisine term that refers to a mince of mushrooms, onions, herbs (such as thyme or parsley), and black pepper, sautéed in butter and reduced to a paste. Cream is sometimes used, and some recipes add a dash of madeira or sherry."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-duxelles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duxelles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish